<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mirror">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Tools and materials</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirror</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6000 BCE</span> · Also known as Looking glass</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mirror is an object that reflects an image. The earliest manufactured mirrors were pieces of polished stone such as obsidian, and examples found at Çatalhöyük in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> have been dated to around 6000 BCE. Mirrors of polished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> were made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> from 4000 BCE and in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from around 3000 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> bronze mirrors were made from around 2000 BCE, some of the earliest by the Qijia culture. Metal mirrors remained the norm until the German chemist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justus-von-liebig">Justus von Liebig</a> is credited with the silvered-glass mirror in 1835.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirror.jpg" alt="Mirror" width="250" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cgs · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mirror.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
